description:
src/extract.rs:210-215:
    #[allow(dead_code)]
    const _: fn() = || {
        let _: fn(Ia5String) -> Ia5String = core::convert::identity;
    };

The accompanying comment says 'Compile-time bridge so the unused-import lint doesn't fire if the Ia5String reference becomes purely documentary in a refactor.'

But Ia5String IS already used in src/extract.rs/tests (`Ia5String::new(*u).unwrap()` line 220 of tests/integration_async.rs and similar in extract.rs tests). It's brought into scope via `use der::{asn1::Ia5String, Decode};` at the top. The const _ creates a use of Ia5String in non-test code — outside #[cfg(test)] — to suppress the dead_code warning that would fire if tests were not compiled. But the lint that needs suppressing is unused_imports, not dead_code, and the suppression mechanism is wrong:

(a) The cleanest fix: drop the const _ entirely, gate the import on cfg(test):
    #[cfg(test)] use der::asn1::Ia5String;
    use der::Decode;

(b) Or remove Ia5String from the use entirely; tests can import it themselves:
    use der::Decode;
    // tests reach it via der::asn1::Ia5String directly

The current shape looks like the author wanted to keep the use idiomatic-looking and hide the test-only-ness behind a no-op const. Net negative: the const is unfamiliar Rust (a const fn() = || { ... } that uses but doesn't call identity), takes 6 lines including the comment, and obscures the fact that the import is test-only. Rewrite: option (b) — three lines deleted, one #[cfg(test)] added on the import that introduces Ia5String.
